💡 **SUGGESTION: cancel parity gap — the structured success short-circuit never checks the run token**

The new guard on the fall-through (this hunk) closes the `ok: false` swallow shape, and the `timedOut` path is already covered transitively (`applyLocalTrimFallback` throws on a cancelled token). One sibling terminal path in the same function is still unguarded: the structured **success short-circuit** at `auto_compactor.dart:1217-1224` —

```dart
if (outcome.ok && (adapter.passCount > 0 || !(force || capTriggered))) {
  hooks.onDone(adapter.passCount, _requestTokens());
  return true;
}
```

A cancel landing after the structured engine settles `ok: true` (e.g. during the final `_refreshState`/verdict, or between the engine's return and this `return true`) reports **success on an aborted run**. The classic engine deliberately defends exactly this race in its `run()` `finally` (`auto_compactor.dart:402-411`): *"a success that races a late cancel is equally dead"* → it throws. Here the abort still surfaces one hop later (the next provider request rides the cancelled token), so this is contract parity, not a user-visible bug — but it leaves the same #1085-shaped hole this PR exists to close, in the same function.

Suggested: hoist the check so every terminal path passes through it, e.g. re-check right after `_runStructured` resolves (before the short-circuit), or add `runToken?.throwIfCancelled();` as the first line of the success branch. The existing `gh-1743` test in `auto_compactor_structured_budget_test.dart` ("a cancelled run token surfaces the abort — the rescue valve never trims… nor reports success") states the contract the short-circuit should honor too.

